2020-- Alex, after the agent version was measured (27,000 input tokens per idea, a quarter of them a catalog re-sent every turn, the same mapped count as one call): "But isn't this return very, very inefficient in terms of the context bloat? … Measure it and realistically tell me if this is the right way." Then, shown the split: "不是本来就应该这样做吗？"
2021-- Decided: `POST /ideas` asks the model twice and nothing else. Call 1 breaks the idea into two to four typed parts (object from the library's object list, what to look for, period, a search query) and reads a dollar claim. The service searches once per part (8 candidates each, id + what it looks at + judged position), follows the ledger's dependency graph from the first part's views to their upstream and downstream views, and evaluates the claim. Call 2 maps each part to its candidates and writes the summary; the service admits only candidate ids. The agent loop (D134) is removed from the service; the MCP front keeps the recipe for an external agent, with the object list instead of a catalog. Measured on the first three ideas: 2,640–2,750 input tokens per idea against 27,000.
2022-- Confirmed by Alex after the 49-probe comparison (5 views mapped per idea against 2, 2,704 input tokens against 27,048): "可以，你确认这个方向是对的，先定论，然后再继续想要怎么优化 1. 速度 2. 成本 3. 质量和稳定的结果". The split is the design; what follows is tuning, each change measured against a fresh control on the same ideas.
